CP(1)                     Funny CLI Manual                     CP(1)

NAME
    cp — Copy files and directories, because sometimes Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V
    aren't available.

SYNOPSIS
    cp <src> <dst>

DESCRIPTION
    The cp command copies a file or directory from a source path to a
    destination path.

    Files are copied along with their metadata. Directories and all of
    their contents are copied recursively.

    Parent directories of the destination are created automatically when
    copying a file.

USAGE
    > cp <src> <dst>       # Copies the source to the destination.

FUN FACTS
    - cp stands for "copy", not "Ctrl+P".
    - Directories bring everything inside them along for the journey.
    - Copying a file does not remove the original.
    - If the destination needs a parent directory, cp creates it for you.

EXAMPLES
    > cp notes.txt backup.txt
        Copies notes.txt to backup.txt.

    > cp notes.txt backups/notes.txt
        Copies notes.txt into the backups directory, creating the
        directory if necessary.

    > cp documents documents_backup
        Copies the documents directory and everything inside it.

    > cp /home/user/config.txt /home/user/backup/config.txt
        Copies config.txt to the backup directory.

SEE ALSO
    help(1), ls(1), mv(1), rm(1), mkdir(1), man(1)

    Remember, cp makes a copy.
    The original stays exactly where you left it.

